The Shining Throne is the 3rd largest political entity of the game's setting, with about 10 stellar systems with cities in  the year 700. It has more systems than Those-Who-Share, but it has less population and a smaller economy.

NAMING
Officially the "Flamboyant Empire of the Earthean Civilisation Under The Shining Throne", it accepts the denomination of "The Shining Throne" but is uncompromising on that "The", similar as The Gambia in real Earth. For internal game documentation we use TST, but even its detractors would rather use "Shining Throne" (intentionally dropping the "The").

1. HISTORY

Formally founded on 578-12-25 by Her Shining Highness, Empress Brunhild the First Baubeiter.

The Golden Throne is the most expansive entity thanks to its cult to heroism. New settlements appear everywhere, nobles go into hybernation to explore new stellar systems, hardships are welcome as a way to prove your worth. They have also an artistic production and "soft power" way above their numbers. Their soap operas, long dramas in hundreds or even thousands of chapters, are admired and loved across the whole mankind - even by many detractors.

2. POLITICS
* The far roots of its ideology are the Romantic movement in PreDis (real life) XIX Century Earth, modulated by the mainstream humanist and liberal consensus of later times, and by the (game setting) movement of Return, which advocated for the return to Earth as centre of mankind - now transferred to Illuminaria, the capital planet, which is being terraformed for that purpose.
* Its vision is double. First, to inspire mankind to greatness, to provide spiritual nourishment with beauty, to achieve cultural excellence touching all aspects of life. Famously, even the cutlery must be beautiful.
* Second, to have the rulers follow chivalrous behaviour, where "protecting the weak" takes the principal role. There is nothing more honourable than having weak to protect and doing it effectively.
* Society is strictly divided, with each rank having its own duties and rights, but social mobility is possible,
even if not encouraged. An exception are foreigners, which customary start in the lowest rank (slave) but are supposed to rapidly climb to their "natural station", which is usually servant, follower or knight.

3. SOCIAL CLASSES
A description of the ranks, also called "stations" follows:
  * Slaves still have the right to be nourished by their owners and not to be physically permanently harmed, which includes basic healthcare. They must obey any order any time, as long as it does not contradict this. In practice, cruelty and severe abuse are also very rare, as they are the kind of behaviour which can provoke a follower or knight to be demoted for not being generous / chivalrous. They are about 5% of population and usually former criminals or volunteers - it may seem strange that someone volunteers to be a slave, but in space, air, water and food cannot be taken for granted, and so the obligation to nourish is very relevant.
  * Serfs have private property and free time, they still have to be nourished and cannot be sexually harassed or tortured in any way. They must work for their Sire in the place and role assigned, but the working times are limited and inclde free days and vacations. They are about 30% of the population.
  * Servants do not have to be nourished, but still protected and helped on need. They must serve their Sire in times of need for specific tasks, but else are free to move and work where they want. They are the first rank to have social obligations towards others: a basic generosity and dignity. They should also behave with certain propriety. A serf can walk drunk on the streets at night. A servant should not. They are about 40% of the population.
  * Followers are responsible of their own nourishment, and pay taxes to the Sire. They are usually owners of business or highly skilled specialists. Some of them are really wealthy. They can be summoned and commanded to do a specific short-time task. This task can be heavy, risky or consequential ("stop selling this", "provide me all your stock of that") but not long. They must behave and dress "with dignity". They are about 20% of the population.
  * The aristocracy is the rest 5%. They are divided in ranks: Knight, Sire, Baron(ess), Count(ess), Duke / Duchess, King / Queen, Emperor / Empress. The higher the rank, the more the following rules apply:
      * They must protect the weak inside the Empire or belonging to the Empire.
      * They must keep their word.
      * They must behave in an elegant, dignified way in public.
      * They must dress in an inspiring way. Not only elegant, but creative.
      * They must know and practice arts.
      * They must provide beauty to their people and to the Empire.
      * They must do heroic, extraordinary things.
      Aristocracy is not de iure hereditary, all children are born "slaves" in the eyes of the law (and so, physical punishment is allowed under the same laws). But the criteria for rising in the ranks are tuned in a way that the child of a high aristrocrat, merely by going to an elite school, and elite university and doing the usual service to the community meanwhile, can reach already the rank of knight by their 18, and reach the status of their parents when they die, inheriting the title.
      The child of an aristocrat is called "na-" when their reach the rank. When the parents die, one child inherits the "demense" ("of the High Mountain"). There are more complex rules around this managing many demenses and more.

* A minority considers that the elite is becoming, by breeding and education, a new and better kind of "super-humanity".

4. CRITICISM
Points which are criticised overseas and also by their own rebels are:
* The very existence of slavery. Nuance is there skipped as the idea iself is considered disgusting.
* Social stratification. How hard it is to raise in the ranks, how hereditary nobility is.
* An "unpractical" way of life, welcoming hardships instead of resolving them, or focusing on beauty instead of survival and prosperity.
* The perceived arrogance of their whole population, slaves included. A certain "superiority complex".
* Their faith that they are the actual future of mankind and the other entities will fail and eventually "bend the knee to The Shining Throne".
* Their love for bombastic, flamboyant and eccentric expresions is often ridiculed.

4.1. Urban legends
These urban legends are believed by some in other political entities:
* Slaves have no rights, they can be killed, tortured and raped at will: false, even if the pressure to accept sexual relations proposed by an owner is real.
* Low classes cannot enjoy the beauty the society produces, access is restricted to the aristocracy: false, even if private (not public) gardens of private mansions exist, the reality is that letting the lower classes participate in beauty is a cornerstone of The Shining Throne.
* Ideas like "helping the weak" are pure propaganda, the aristocracy is selfish and cruel: false, aristocracy is educated in these values and they watch each other to ensure compliance. However, this vigilance is not perfect, is weak behind closed doors, and abuses and classism do exist, particularly in some minorities.
* They want to conquer mankind: false, but inspired by the fact that they think the other systems will fail and they will prevail. But there is no appetite for doing this through war and violence. Those are ugly. They will simply wait.
* The lower classes hate the aristocracy and want to rebel: false, the huge majority loves and admires their Sires, even if is in part provoked by their ability to hide their true feelings and acts.

5. ECONOMY
By one side, they have become experts in what they call "the mere act of survival", that is: life support systems, hydroponics and exposure to extreme environments. But the other side, they export all kinds of artisanship and luxurious items, and also cultural production.
Else, it is a quite "rural society", meaning small settlements scattered over large territories.

6. DAILY LIFE
* Everybody lives 'under' a Sire or a small groups of Sires. Visiting botanic gardens, promenades, museums, open-air theatres and festivals are common free-time activities, available for everybody. Statues, banners and heraldy are everywhere.
* Everyhing is decorated in some way: facades and street lights, food and instruments (unless imported), vehicles and yes, cutlery. Explicitly: this includes things destined to be user by ALL ranks, the rank only affects the richness and value of the decoration.
* Education is stratified, with only servants and above receiving any free one, and only followers and above getting university for free. Also, there are elite schools and universities, reserved for the nobility.

6.1. FAMILY
* During pregnancy it is established who will care about the child. The biological mother has preference, the biological father comes next, then anybody else. At the end, all "three parts" of responsibility must be assigned. This decision is irrevocable. If nobody is found to cover all three thirds, the "Crown" (=the State) takes over and finds new parents. However, rejecting responsibility over own biological children is extremely shunned in society and very rare.
* Stable families, where all children have the same responsible people and live together, are encouraged and protected by law.
* Lineage is important and classism is rampant. People are not expected to raise over the station of their parents. However, if someone behaves the part, the doors are open. There is thus a big difference between expectation (none) and acceptance (high).